# Options to configure the Bazel Server in CI to be stricter in terms of RAM consumption, at the cost of speed of analysis in subsequent
# runs.
#
# See `BAZEL_CI_STARTUP_OPTIONS` in the TeamCity DSL.
# No workspace `.bazelrc` imports it.
# It should not be used locally either.

# The server stops after each command.
#
# TODO: remove and replace with shutting down the server at appropriated times in TeamCity, the server will have to be stopped
# TODO:  - after every group of steps that re-used the server (server are shared following some things like working dirs, etc.)
# TODO:  - at the end of the build configuration that spawned it
# TODO:  - at worst by an aggressive --max_idle_secs flag
startup --batch

# A short-lived server needs less memory than the server of `common.bazelrc`.
# On the Windows staging run of MRI-5920, 6 GB ran out of memory and 7 GB passed.
# A `--host_jvm_args=-Xmx` on the command line comes after this one, so the heap of a build that sets its own stays.
# Keep it in sync with the `-Xmx` of `common.bazelrc`: the heap of `common.bazelrc` minus the size of the analysis cache.
# Today it is 10 - 3 = 7.
startup --host_jvm_args=-Xmx7g

# The next command starts a new server, so the state of this one has no use.
# A `common` line skips an option that the command does not support: `query` and `mod` skip `--discard_analysis_cache`.
common --notrack_incremental_state
common --discard_analysis_cache
common --nokeep_state_after_build

# `cquery` and `aquery` read the analysis, so they need the incremental state.
cquery --track_incremental_state
aquery --track_incremental_state
